Report the PTC backtest as money instead of p-values

The backtest was run and reported, but as significance tests, which failed to
answer the actual question: would this have made money? Adding
research/ptc_show_me_the_money.py, which reports the identical replay - 303
real orders, real fill timestamps, real prices, real settlements - in dollars,
trade by trade.

THE ANSWER, Aug 4-5, q15:

  arm                  Aug 4     Aug 5     TOTAL
  CONTROL            +101.28    -98.32     +2.96
  PROBE_ONLY_60        +7.59     -7.01     +0.58
  PTC_60               +9.63     -6.76     +2.87
  PTC_120             +13.83     -4.95     +8.88

PTC_120 beat the existing strategy 3x on the two live days. That should have
been the headline and was not.

HOW it won matters more than that it won. Control beat PTC badly on Aug 4
(+101 vs +14) and collapsed on Aug 5 (-98 vs -5). PTC's daily range was $18
against control's $200. It won by not losing on the bad day, not by earning
on the good one - a risk property, not an edge. On a different pair of days
where the good day is larger, control wins.

WHY IT IS STILL NOT PROVEN. The same replay with one rule changed:

  as run                          +8.88
  ask ceiling 78c instead of 80c  +3.08
  ask ceiling 82c instead of 80c +11.43
  wait 60s instead of 120s        +2.87
  IOC only half-fills             +4.26

Range +2.87 to +11.43. A two-cent change to one threshold moves the answer 4x,
because six commitments cannot hold it still. The problem is not that the
backtest was skipped; it is that this sample cannot price the branch.

WHAT THE TWO DAYS DO ESTABLISH, on 303 orders rather than 6:

  cancel 60s   filled n=264 win 69.32%   not filled n=39 win 87.18%  (+17.9pp)
  cancel 120s  filled n=270 win 69.26%   not filled n=33 win 90.91%  (+21.6pp)

The signal is measured and solid. Its dollar value is not. Those are different
questions with different sample sizes, and conflating them is what made the
earlier reporting unreadable.

